course: dictionary
lesson: 11414
title: The accusative case
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: In grammar, the accusative is the form of a noun or pronoun used when it is the object of a verb: the one the action is done to.
explain: In «She saw him», the action is seeing, and him is the one being seen. So him is in the accusative.
explain: English shows this mostly with pronouns: me, him, her, us, them, whom. We say «He saw me», not «He saw I», because I is for the one doing the action, not the one receiving it.
fact: The accusative is a case: a form a word takes because of its job in the sentence.
fact: In English, the accusative shows up mainly in pronouns: me, him, her, us, them, whom.
fact: «She saw him.» Him is the object, the one the action is done to.
fact: In German, «der Hund» (the dog) becomes «den Hund» when it is the object: «Ich sehe den Hund.»
fact: Latin, German, Russian and Ukrainian all change word endings for the accusative.
exercise: They invited ___ to the party. | we | our | *us
exercise: In «The cat chased them», what is them? | The one doing the chasing | *The one being chased | The verb
exercise: Which sentence is correct? | «He saw my.» | *«He saw me.» | «He saw I.»
test: Please help ___ with the bags. | *us | our | we
test: In «The dog barked at her», what is her? | The one doing the barking | The verb barked | *The one the barking is aimed at
exam: Which sentence uses the accusative correctly? | «Us went to the park.» | «Me and him went home.» | *«She met us at the gate.»
